# Cases for core-language-docs/functions-records.md. Read by ../run-all.sh,
# which discovers every */CASES under snippets/ rather than carrying a list.
#
# Five pipe-separated fields:
#
#   module | cfg | expected-rc | distinct-states | what it shows
#
# A blank line or a line opening with # is skipped.
#
# THE FOURTH FIELD IS THE STATE COUNT, and on this page it carries two
# findings an rc check cannot see. Grow.cfg reads 4 states and GrowReach.cfg
# reads 1, and that pair IS the claim that EXCEPT one past the end of a
# sequence does not append. Both runs exit 0 and both print the same warning,
# so an rc-only suite would stay green while the page went wrong. Typed.cfg
# reads 9 because a function set holds |T| to the power of |S| members and
# each one is an initial state, which is the only place the page quotes that
# arithmetic against a real run. Leave the field empty where the page quotes
# no count.
#
# Exit codes TLC uses here:
#   0    all checks passed
#   12   an invariant was violated
#   75   a runtime evaluation error, so the run stopped rather than reporting
#   150  parse or semantic error in the module
#
# 75 is not in the modules page's list and it is the one worth knowing about.
# TLC reaches it when an expression cannot be evaluated at all, which is what
# reading outside a function's domain does. The run does not finish, so there
# is no verdict to read either way.

Shape.tla|Shape.cfg|0|3|a record, a function and a sequence are one kind of value
Shape.tla|ShapeSeparate.cfg|12||and the reading that holds a record apart from a function
Merge.tla|Merge.cfg|0|2|:> and @@ build a function, and @@ keeps the left value
Merge.tla|MergeRight.cfg|12||the dictionary-update reading, where the right value wins
Meter.tla|Meter.cfg|0|7|EXCEPT with @, a nested selector, and clauses read left to right
Meter.tla|MeterNaive.cfg|12||the reading where every @ in a clause list sees the original
Grow.tla|Grow.cfg|0|4|Append grows the sequence
Grow.tla|GrowReach.cfg|0|1|EXCEPT one past the end does not append
Typo.tla|Typo.cfg|0|1|a misspelled field name is a dropped clause
Outside.tla|Outside.cfg|75||reading one past the end stops the run
Recurse.tla|Recurse.cfg|0|6|the bracket form calls itself with no RECURSIVE line
RecurseOp.tla|RecurseOp.cfg|150||the operator form of the same definition does not parse
Typed.tla|Typed.cfg|0|9|the colon form is a set, and a function set holds |T|^|S| members
Typed.tla|TypedArrow.cfg|75||the arrow form is one record, so \in has nothing to search
